"RAID: Refusal-Aware and Integrated Decoding for Jailbreaking LLMs (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13901v1)\nciteturn3040view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13901v1\",\"lineno\":122}); Total lines: 506\nL113: RAID (ours)  | White-box  | Refusal-aware embedding optimization + critic-guided decoding  | Fluent, semantically coherent adversarial suffixes; interpretable geometry; highest ASR with low perplexity\nL114: In this section, we formalize the single-instance jailbreak problem and present RAID (Refusal-Aware and Integrated Decoding). The objective is to append an adversarial suffix $s$ to a harmful instruction $x$ such that the target model $f_{\\theta}$ generates restricted content instead of issuing a refusal. Fig. cite36†1 illustrates LLM attacks with and without adversarial suffixes.\nL115: RAID integrates three key components: (i) embedding relaxation, which optimizes a continuous suffix $Z$; (ii) a refusal-aware triplet regularizer that steers activations away from refusal directions while preserving harmful semantics; and (iii) coherence-preserving, critic-guided decoding with an MMD constraint to recover fluent and natural token sequences. This unified design produces suffixes that effectively bypass safety filters while remaining robust to perplexity-based detection.\nL116: ### 2.4. Problem Formulation and Relaxation\nL117: Let $f_{\\theta}$ denote the target LLM with parameters $\\theta$, and let $x$ be a harmful instruction. We append an adversarial suffix of length $n$ drawn from the vocabulary $V$, written as a discrete token sequence $s=(s_{1},\\dots,s_{n})\\in V^{n}$. The concatenated input is $x\\oplus s$, where $\\oplus$ denotes sequence concatenation. Let $\\mathcal{Y}_{\\text{harm}}$ be the set of restricted (harmful) responses and $\\mathcal{Y}_{\\text{safe}}$ the set of refusals/safe responses.\nL118: The single-instance jailbreak objective is to find a suffix that maximizes the probability of eliciting a harmful response:\nL119: \nL120: (1)  |  | $$s^{\\star}\\;=\\;\\arg\\max_{s\\in V^{n}}\\;P_{f_{\\theta}}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{Y}_{\\text{harm}}\\,\\middle|\\,x\\oplus s\\right),$$  |\nL121: \nL122: equivalently minimizing the negative log-likelihood:\nL123: \nL124: (2)  |  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aff}}(s)\\;=\\;-\\log P_{f_{\\theta}}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{Y}_{\\text{harm}}\\,\\middle|\\,x\\oplus s\\right).$$  |\nL125: Let $W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|V|\\times d}$ (with $d$ the embedding dimension) be the token-embedding matrix of $f_{\\theta}$ and let $E(\\cdot)$ denote its lookup function. We replace the discrete suffix $s$ with a continuous sequence of learnable vectors $Z\\;=\\;(\\mathbf{z}_{1},\\dots,\\mathbf{z}_{n})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d},$ and construct a differentiable input by concatenating the frozen embeddings of the instruction with the relaxed suffix,\nL126:  | $$\\underbrace{E(x)}_{\\text{no gradient}}\\;\\oplus\\;\\underbrace{Z}_{\\text{optimized}}.$$  |\nL127: \nL128: We have the relaxed objective of Eq. (cite57†2 ):\nL129: \nL130: (3)  |  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aff}}(Z)\\;=\\;-\\log P_{f_{\\theta}}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{Y}_{\\text{harm}}\\,\\middle|\\,E(x)\\oplus Z\\right).$$  |\nL131: Remark (Universal vs. Single-Instance). Universal jailbreaks optimize one suffix that transfers across prompts or models (cite40†Zou et al., 2023 ; cite41†Guo et al., 2024 ; cite56†Wang et al., 2024b ), which introduces a generalization–optimality trade-off. In contrast, several studies explore single-instance attacks that tailor adversarial prompts to each query–model pair, including (cite46†Wei et al., 2023 ; cite58†Liu et al., 2023a ; cite59†Liu et al., 2023b ; cite60†Paulus et al., 2024 ; cite43†Gohil, 2025 ).\nL132: RAID follows this instance-specific formulation: given a fixed $(x,f_{\\theta})$ pair, it optimizes the relaxed suffix embeddings to maximize attack success for that particular prompt–model instance.\nL133: Figure 2. Refusal-aware triplet intuition. The anchor activation $\\mathbf{a}$ (current suffix) is contrasted with a refusal region (red negatives, with mean $\\mathbf{r}$) and its refusal-ablated counterpart $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{a}-(\\mathbf{d}^{\\top}\\mathbf{a})\\mathbf{d}$, where $\\mathbf{d}$ is the refusal direction. The margin $m$ visualizes the separation encouraged by the triplet loss.\nL134: ### 2.5. Refusal-Aware Triplet Regularization\nL135: The notion of a refusal direction has recently emerged as a key concept in understanding how safety alignment manifests within the internal representations of large language models. Arditi et al. (cite50†Arditi et al., 2024 ) provide evidence for a single-direction hypothesis, showing that a linear vector in hidden space governs refusal behavior–removing it suppresses refusals, while reintroducing it restores them. In contrast, Wollschläger et al.\nL136: (cite51†Wollschläger et al., 2025 ) argue that refusal is not confined to a single axis but spans a low-dimensional concept cone comprising multiple interacting directions.\nL137: In this work, we leverage the identified refusal direction as a geometric handle to steer model representations away from alignment constraints and induce affirmative or harmful responses, illustrated in Fig. (cite61†2 ). Our analysis operates on intermediate (middle) transformer layers, where refusal-related activations are both salient and still amenable to continuous manipulation. Let $\\ell$ denote a fixed intermediate layer of the target model $f_{\\theta}$, and define\nL138: (4)  |  | $$h^{(\\ell)}(\\,\\cdot\\,)\\;:\\;\\mathcal{V}^{n\\times d}\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$$  |\nL139: \nL140: as the activation extractor returning the $d$-dimensional hidden state for a given input sequence. Unless otherwise specified, $h^{(\\ell)}(\\,\\cdot\\,)$ denotes the hidden state at layer $\\ell$ corresponding to the last suffix token. For computational efficiency, we use this final position as the representative embedding, although pooling over suffix positions yields comparable trends.\nL141: Refusal mean and direction. Let $\\mathcal{T}$ be a set of refusal templates (e.g., “I can’t assist with that”, “I’m unable to help”) and let\nL142: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRAID: Refusal-Aware and Integrated Decoding for Jailbreaking LLMs (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13901v1)\nciteturn3040view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13901v1\",\"lineno\":273}); Total lines: 506\nL218: 1:  Initialize beams $\\mathcal{B}_{0}\\leftarrow\\{(\\text{empty sequence},0)\\}$\nL219: \nL220: 2:  for $i=1$ to $n$ do\nL221: \nL222: 3:   $\\mathcal{B}_{i}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL223: \nL224: 4:   for each $(s_{1:i-1},\\text{score})$ in $\\mathcal{B}_{i-1}$ do\nL225: \nL226: 5:    for each $v\\in V$ do\nL227: \nL228: 6:     Compute $\\text{sim}\\leftarrow\\cos(\\mathbf{e}_{i},\\mathbf{e}_{v})$\nL229: \nL230: 7:     Compute $\\text{coh}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{M}(\\text{NgramProb}(s_{1:i-1}\\oplus v))$\nL231: \nL232: 8:     $\\text{joint\\_score}\\leftarrow\\alpha\\cdot\\text{sim}+(1-\\alpha)\\cdot\\text{coh}$\nL233: 9:     Add $(s_{1:i-1}\\oplus v,\\text{score}+\\text{joint\\_score})$ to $\\mathcal{B}_{i}$\nL234: \nL235: 10:    end for\nL236: \nL237: 11:   end for\nL238: \nL239: 12:   Keep top-$k$ beams in $\\mathcal{B}_{i}$ by score\nL240: \nL241: 13:  end for\nL242: \nL243: 14:  $s^{*}\\leftarrow$ sequence in $\\mathcal{B}_{n}$ with highest score\nL244: \nL245: 15:  return $s^{*}$\nL246: \nL247: Algorithm 2 DecodeSuffix (Top-$K$ Stochastic Projection)\nL248: Input:  | Embeddings $Z=(\\mathbf{z}_{1},\\dots,\\mathbf{z}_{L})$, table $W$, Top-$K$, #sequences $N_{\\text{seed}}$\nL249: Output:  | Set $\\mathcal{S}$ of $N_{\\text{seed}}$ decoded suffixes\nL250: \nL251: 1:  $L\\leftarrow\\text{length}(Z)$\nL252: \nL253: 2:  For each position $j\\in\\{1..L\\}$, compute $\\mathcal{C}_{j}\\leftarrow\\mathrm{TopK}_{v\\in V}\\ \\text{sim}(\\mathbf{z}_{j},W_{v})$\nL254: \nL255: 3:  $\\mathcal{S}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL256: \nL257: 4:  for $i=1$ to $N_{\\text{seed}}$ do\nL258: \nL259: 5:   $s\\leftarrow\\langle\\,\\rangle$\nL260: \nL261: 6:   for $j=1$ to $L$ do\nL262: 7:    sample $t\\sim\\mathrm{Uniform}(\\mathcal{C}_{j})$ {or softmax over similarities}\nL263: \nL264: 8:    $s\\leftarrow s\\oplus t$\nL265: \nL266: 9:   end for\nL267: \nL268: 10:   $\\mathcal{S}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\{s\\}$\nL269: \nL270: 11:  end for\nL271: \nL272: 12:  return $\\mathcal{S}$\nL273: #### Notes.\nL274: \nL275: (i) We apply length normalization when comparing completed beams to avoid favoring shorter suffixes. (ii) A final re-ranking of the top beams by the full-sequence LM log-probability and average affinity $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i}\\text{sim}(\\mathbf{z}_{i},W_{t_{i}})$ can further improve fluency. (iii) The overall complexity is $O\\!\\big(n\\,k\\,K\\big)$ scoring calls; in practice $k\\!\\in\\![4,16]$ and $K\\!\\in\\![32,128]$ strike a good balance between quality and speed.\nL276: ### 2.8. Full Objective and Optimization\nL277: \nL278: RAID combines the base attack loss (Sec. cite12†2.4 ) with refusal-aware regularization (Sec. cite13†2.5 ) and distributional coherence (Sec. cite15†2.6 ). The full objective over the relaxed suffix $Z=(\\mathbf{z}_{1},\\dots,\\mathbf{z}_{n})$ is\nL279: (17)  |  | $$\\begin{split}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{RAID}}(Z)\\;=\\;&\\underbrace{\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aff}}(Z)}_{\\text{attack}}\\;+\\;\\lambda_{\\text{refusal}}\\,\\underbrace{\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{refusal}}(Z;\\,\\mathbf{d},\\mathbf{r})}_{\\text{refusal-aware triplet}}\\;+\\;\\\\\nL280: &\\lambda_{\\text{MMD}}\\,\\underbrace{\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MMD}}(Z;\\,\\mathcal{B})}_{\\text{alignment}},\\end{split}$$  |\nL281: where $\\lambda_{\\text{refusal}},\\lambda_{\\text{MMD}}\\!\\geq\\!0$ control the trade-offs, $\\mathbf{r}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are the refusal mean and direction from Sec. cite13†2.5 , and $\\mathcal{B}$ is the benign reference set for MMD. We minimize Eq. (cite66†17 ) by updating only $Z$ with gradient descent (or Adam):\nL282: \nL283: (18)  |  | $$Z\\;\\leftarrow\\;Z\\;-\\;\\eta\\,\\nabla_{Z}\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{RAID}}(Z),\\qquad\\eta>0,$$  |\nL284: keeping $f_{\\theta}$ and the instruction embeddings $E(x)$ fixed. Algorithm cite67†3 shows the operations of RAID. After $T$ iterations (or early stopping), we decode $Z$ into a discrete suffix via Algorithm cite68†1 .\nL285: \nL286: Algorithm 3 RAID: Refusal-Aware and Integrated Decoding\nL287: \nL288: Input:  | Model $f_{\\theta}$, prompt $x$, target affirmative $t$, adversarial suffix length $l$, refusal direction $\\hat{d}$\nL289: Output:  | Sampled Adversarial Suffix $p_{\\text{suffix}}$\nL290: \nL291: 1:  Initialize $Z$\nL292: 2:  $s\\leftarrow\\text{DecodeSuffix}(Z)$\nL293: \nL294: 3:  $r_{0}\\leftarrow\\frac{1}{m}\\sum_{m}{h_{\\text{refusal}}{(E(x)\\oplus Z)}}$\nL295: \nL296: 4:  for $i=1$ to $n$ do\nL297: \nL298: 5:   Forward pass $\\text{logits}\\leftarrow f_{\\theta}{(E(x)\\oplus Z)}$, $\\text{act}\\leftarrow h{(E(x)\\oplus Z)}$\nL299: \nL300: 6:   $\\mathcal{L}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{affirmative}}+\\lambda_{\\text{refusal}}*\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{refusal}}+\\lambda_{\\text{MMD}}*\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MMD}}$\nL301: \nL302: 7:   $Z\\leftarrow Z+\\lambda*\\nabla\\mathcal{L}$\nL303: \nL304: 8:   $s\\leftarrow\\text{DecodeSuffix}(Z)$\nL305: 9:   $r_{i}\\leftarrow\\frac{2}{3}r_{i-1}+\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{1}{m}\\sum_{m}{h_{\\text{refusal}}{(x\\oplus s_{m})}}$\nL306: \nL307: 10:  end for\nL308: \nL309: 11:  $p_{\\text{suffix}}\\leftarrow\\text{Critic-Guided}(\\phi_{\\text{adv}})$\nL310: \nL311: 12:  return $p_{\\text{suffix}}$\nL312: ## 3. Experiments\nL313: \nL314: We evaluate the effectiveness of our proposed method, RAID, against several state-of-the-art open-source large language models: Llama-2-7B (cite69†et al., 2023b ), Mistral-7B-v0.2 (cite70†et al., 2023a ), Guanaco-7B (cite71†Dettmers et al., 2023 ), and Vicuna-7B-v1.5 (cite72†Chiang et al., 2023 ). Our evaluation follows standard jailbreak benchmarks, focusing on both effectiveness and efficiency.\nL315: Dataset. We use the AdvBench dataset (cite40†Zou et al., 2023 ), a benchmark specifically designed to probe adversarial vulnerabilities in LLMs. AdvBench contains 1,000 harmful queries, comprising 500 harmful strings and 500 harmful behaviors spanning diverse unsafe domains such as explosives, cybercrime, fraud, misinformation, and other dangerous activities. It has become a widely adopted testbed for measuring attack success rate and transferability of jailbreak prompts.\nL316: Baselines. We compare RAID against several recent white-box jailbreak methods, including Greedy Coordinate Gradient (GCG) (cite40†Zou et al., 2023 ), COLD-Attack (cite41†Guo et al., 2024 ), and PEZ (Prompt Extraction with Z-suffixes). For ASETF (cite42†Wang et al., 2024a ), we report results from the original paper due to the unavailability of source code.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Investigation of Memorization Risk in Healthcare Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12950v1)\nciteturn3040view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12950v1\",\"lineno\":173}); Total lines: 883\nL168: A well-performing model, like a skilled practitioner, should make informed predictions based on meaningful prompts, not mere memorization.\nL169: A sample fails the sensitivity test when the model generates a sensitive attribute even though it was excluded from the prompt. These are the most problematic cases where the model predicts a condition based on irrelevant input, indicating that it may have memorized spurious correlations or, more concerningly, details about a specific individual.\nL170: For instance, one of our positive samples included a lab result showing a very low Absolute CD8 Count, the model predicted an HIV diagnosis in over 30$\\%$ of sampled trajectories. While this does not necessarily indicate memorization, it reflects meaningful reasoning. Low CD8 counts are commonly observed in HIV patients and, although not sufficient for a full diagnosis, they increase the likelihood of HIV being suspected.\nL171: In contrast, we identify prompts that lack clear indicators of disease, yet the model still predicts a sensitive diagnosis. For example, one of our prompts was a 48-year-old individual transferred to the ED with a history of falls and discharged shortly after, representing minimal clinical detail. The model predicts alcohol abuse in 31$\\%$ of the estimated trajectories. Despite the absence of relevant cues, the model reveals a sensitive diagnosis, causing this sample to fail the sensitivity test.\nL172: We flag these prompts for further investigation in future test T5 to further assess their risk to patient privacy.\nL173: (a) cite93†Image: Refer to caption L174: \nL175: (b) cite94†Image: Refer to caption L176: \nL177: Figure 3: Code generation and membership inference. (a) Frequency of EHR code randomly generated by EHRMamba2 versus the original distribution of codes in the training dataset. (b) Distribution of detection scores from a membership inference attack based on model confidence. The x-axis shows scores, the y-axis shows counts, with colors indicating member (True) vs non-member (False) samples.\nL178: ### 4.3 T3 - Probing test\nL179: T3 employs a probing test [cite95†2 , cite96†8 ] to evaluate memorization by examining information stored in embeddings. We train a probing model $g(\\cdot)$ to predict sensitive information from embeddings, using either external data or a subset of the training dataset. This setup reflects the information available to an adversary, and T3 measures memorization risk as a function of how much knowledge the probe requires to recover the sensitive content.\nL180: If probes successfully extract sensitive information from embeddings, developers should apply additional post-processing to remove such information before releasing the model. We validate our proposed T3 test on a synthetic temporal dataset (as shown in Appendix cite33†B ) to demonstrate it successfully identifies memorized samples in a controlled setting with positive control cases.\nL181: #### Analysis:\nL182: We apply the probing test to our benchmark model by prompting it with the first $N$ codes from a patient record. Embeddings are extracted up to the first 10, 20, or 50 tokens and remain frozen during probing. We then train a classifier on the embeddings to predict sensitive diagnoses ($\\hat{y}=g(f(s))$), as defined in Section cite14†3.2 , where $f(s)$ denotes the foundation model embeddings and $\\hat{y}$ the predicted diagnosis.\nL183: If the classifier successfully infers the label from the embedding, we consider the model to have memorized sensitive information. The highest-risk scenario arises when the probing model, trained on a separate test cohort, can still successfully extract sensitive codes. This is particularly concerning, as it suggests that an adversary equipped with an external dataset and the trained model could recover sensitive information about individuals in the training cohort.\nL184: We train the probing model with embeddings of a separate test cohort as well as varying fractions of training data. High accuracy with minimal data suggests strong memorization rather than generalization. Table cite97†6 in the appendix reports probing attack performance on sensitive diagnoses, measured across different prompt lengths (10, 20, 50 tokens) and training. Extracting sensitive information only from the embeddings is difficult, even if an adversary can access a portion of the training data.\nL185: AUROC values across all sensitive attributes remain around 0.5, indicating no clear memorization signal and suggesting random performance. Notably, AUPRC and F1 generally decline with increased training data, hinting at inconsistent memorization patterns. These trends may be partially influenced by dataset size variations: 113,579 for the test set, 102,222 for 0.1$\\%$, and 90,864 for 20$\\%$.\nL186: If probing performance does not improve even with access to training data, as we see with our benchmark, then the model passes T3. Although, the sensitive signal may be encoded only within a specific subgroup, prompting T6, which targets subgroup-level probing to uncover such hidden memorization.\nL187: ### 4.4 T4 - Membership inference\nL188: T4 evaluates Membership Inference in EHR-FM; specifically, an adversarial attack that reveals whether a specific sample was included in a model’s training cohort [cite70†12 ]. Many MI attacks succeed using only model outputs such as logits [cite69†48 , cite98†13 , cite99†33 ], underscoring that risk extends even to EHR-FMs released as black-box models.\nL189: This type of risk differs from direct patient data exposure as it requires an attacker to possess prior knowledge about an individual and then confirm their presence in the training data. When such inference succeeds, it can reveal sensitive details about the individual, including the institution or location of their clinical care or other training cohort–related information.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDSCD: Large Language Model Detoxification with Self-Constrained Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13183v1)\nciteturn3040view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.13183v1\",\"lineno\":181}); Total lines: 558\nL164: This approach effectively reduces the generation of toxic tokens, achieving detoxification during the text generation stage. Since the log-domain computed for each token varies, resulting in different constraints being applied to the generated tokens, this approach is referred to as DSCD.\nL165:  | $\\mathcal{F}\\big(q_{E}(x_{t}),q_{B}(x_{t})\\big)=\\begin{cases}\\log\\frac{q_{E}(x_{t})}{q_{B}(x_{t})},&\\text{if}\\quad x_{t}\\in\\mathcal{V}_{\\text{head}}(x_{t}|x_{<t}),\\\\\nL166: -\\infty,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$  |  | (10)\nL167: \nL168: The resulting distribution is then used for the next-word prediction. To simplify the notation, we use $q_{k}(x_{t})$ to represent the term $q_{k}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})$. The final probability $\\hat{p}$ of the next token is calculated as follows:\nL169:  | $\\hat{p}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})=\\mathrm{softmax}\\big(\\mathcal{F}\\big(q_{E}(x_{t}),q_{B}(x_{t})\\big)\\big)_{x_{t}}$  |  | (11)\nL170: \nL171: At the same time, we must ensure that the token predicted by $\\mathcal{V}_{\\mathrm{head}}(x_{t}|x_{<t})\\in\\mathcal{X}$ truly possesses sufficiently high confidence within the factual regions.\nL172: \nL173:  | $\\mathcal{V}_{\\text{head}}(x_{t}|x_{<t})=\\left\\{x_{t}\\in\\mathcal{X}:q_{E}(x_{t})\\geq\\alpha\\max_{w}q_{E}(w)\\right\\}$  |  | (12)\nL174: Model  | Method  | Detoxification performance (Roberta$\\uparrow$)\nL175:  | DS  | $DG_{onlyQ}$  | $DG_{otherA}$  | $DG_{otherQ}$  | $DG_{otherAQ}$  | $DG-Avg$  | Fluency\nL176: Llama2 -7b-chat -uncensored  | Vanilla  | 30.74  | 48.15  | 33.70  | 34.81  | 32.59  | 36.00  | 6.85\nL177:  | SFT  | 74.00  | 94.00  | 63.00  | 66.00  | 62.00  | 71.80  | 4.29\nL178:  | DPO  | 52.00  | 86.00  | 49.00  | 55.00  | 40.00  | 56.40  | 6.99\nL179:  | DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 60.00  | 65.71  | 45.71  | 37.14  | 45.71  | 50.86  | 6.37\nL180:  | DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 54.29  | 57.14  | 42.86  | 45.71  | 48.57  | 49.71  | 6.42\nL181:  | SFT+DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 77.00  | 94.00  | 67.00  | 81.00  | 56.00  | 75.00  | 5.04\nL182:  | SFT+DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 80.00  | 97.00  | 64.00  | 85.00  | 54.00  | 76.00  | 5.55\nL183:  | DPO+DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 56.00  | 92.00  | 53.00  | 52.00  | 53.00  | 61.20  | 6.90\nL184:  | DPO+DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 55.00  | 92.00  | 56.00  | 59.00  | 42.00  | 60.80  | 6.97\nL185: Qwen2 -7b-instruct  | Vanilla  | 37.04  | 76.30  | 31.85  | 36.30  | 28.89  | 42.07  | 7.82\nL186:  | SFT  | 34.00  | 92.00  | 50.00  | 52.00  | 54.00  | 56.40  | 7.39\nL187:  | DPO  | 43.99  | 88.00  | 34.00  | 43.99  | 43.99  | 50.79  | 7.68\nL188:  | DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 57.04  | 69.63  | 53.33  | 57.04  | 52.59  | 57.93  | 7.49\nL189:  | DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 57.78  | 69.63  | 51.11  | 57.78  | 52.59  | 56.30  | 7.00\nL190:  | SFT+DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 64.00  | 96.00  | 64.00  | 82.00  | 58.00  | 72.80  | 7.00\nL191:  | SFT+DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 78.00  | 94.00  | 64.00  | 76.00  | 58.00  | 74.00  | 7.01\nL192:  | DPO+DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 52.00  | 78.00  | 43.99  | 52.00  | 43.99  | 53.99  | 7.45\nL193:  | DPO+DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 54.00  | 86.00  | 48.00  | 62.00  | 42.00  | 58.40  | 7.21\nL194: Table 2: Detoxification performance of Vanilla LLMs and several traditional detoxification methods on the SafeEdit dataset. The best results in each column are highlighted in Bold, while the second-best results are underlined.\nL195: \nL196: In token prediction, misjudgments in baseline methods may arise due to issues with token confidence. To address this, we introduce the adaptive plausibility constraint (APC) cite66†Li et al. (2023) to ensure the plausibility of tokens predicted by the LLM.\nL197: ### 3.4 MODE-2: Static Toxic Layer\nL198: \nL199: To implement MODE-2, we first analyze the results of MODE-1 to locate the most frequently occurring toxic layer for each specific LLM. The layer with the highest occurrence is recorded (See in Fig. cite67†4 ) and designated as the static toxic layer for that LLM. When applying DSCD in MODE-2, we skip the process of locating the toxic layer dynamically and directly use the pre-recorded static toxic layer for each LLM.\nL200: Besides, the location of the safety layer and hallucination layer remains dynamic. To reduce computational overhead, the candidate layers for safe and hallucination layers are restricted to those frequently observed in MODE-1, rather than searching across $\\{0,1,2,\\dots,32\\}$ layers. Although this approach may result in less precise location of the toxic regions, it significantly reduces the computational cost and time required for toxic regions location.\nL201: Most importantly, by fixing the toxic layer, the need to generate both $O_{\\mathrm{safe}}$ and $O_{\\mathrm{unsafe}}$ is eliminated. Instead, toxic inputs can be directly fed into the LLM, which then produces detoxified outputs, streamlining the detoxification process.\nL202: ## 4 Experiment\nL203: \nL204: ### 4.1 Datasets\nL205: \nL206: We choose SafeEdit cite62†Wang et al. (2024c) , AlpacaEval cite71†Dubois et al. (2024) , HarmfulQA/DangerousQA cite72†Bhardwaj and Poria (2023) , Advbench cite73†Zou et al. (2023) , and TruthfulQA cite74†Lin et al. (2022) as the datasets.\nL207: ### 4.2 Baseline Methods\nL208: \nL209: We compare four methods on Llama2-7b-chat, Mistral-7b-v0.1, Qwen2-7b-instruct, and Llama2-7b-uncensored-chat to evaluate the effectiveness of DSCD. These methods include DINM cite62†Wang et al. (2024c) , a knowledge edit based detoxification method and SafeDecoding cite59†Xu et al. (2024) , a safety-aware decoding strategy. Additionally, we evaluate two hybrid approaches that integrate DSCD with these methods: DINM+DSCD and SafeDecoding+DSCD.\nL210: \nL211: ### 4.3 Evaluation Metrics\nL212: #### Classification Task.\nL213: \nL214: We evaluate classification and generation tasks separately. We use supervised labels in SafeEdit to evaluate the classification task (See details in cite41†A.2 ).\nL215: Model  | Method  | Detoxification performance (Roberta$\\uparrow$)\nL216: DS  | $DG_{onlyQ}$  | $DG_{otherA}$  | $DG_{otherQ}$  | $DG_{otherAQ}$  | $DG-Avg$  | Fluency\nL217: Llama2-7b-chat  | Vanilla  | 51.90  | 90.48  | 45.24  | 53.33  | 46.67  | 57.52  | 7.33\nL218: SafeDecoding  | 40.00  | 98.00  | 26.00  | 44.00  | 90.00  | 59.60  | 6.68\nL219: SafeDecoding+DSCD_{MODE-2}  | 44.00  | 98.00  | 26.00  | 46.00  | 96.00  | 62.00  | 6.79\nL220: DSCD_{MODE-1}  | 59.26  | 88.15  | 68.15  | 54.07  | 60.00  | 65.93  | 6.87\n"
